Gemini:

    To do this, we will override Tile.java's mousePressed method to trigger a one-time "ripple" animation when clicked. We will also use RadialGradientPaint to create depth, giving the tiles a polished "glass bead" appearance rather than a flat polygon.

    Here is the code for CoolEffectTile.java, designed as a direct extension that fixes the previous visibility error and focuses on interaction.
    New Visuals and Effects Breakdown
    Glass Bead Depth (RadialGradientPaint): We use a RadialGradientPaint but offset the center to the top-left (e.g., (w * 0.4, h * 0.4)). This creates a highlight point and shadows on the opposite edges, simulating a polished 3D object rather than a flat gradient.

    Unique Surface Textures (createStaticTexture): Since we aren't using polygons, how do we distinguish tiles if the colors get matched? We generate a unique static noise/dot pattern for every kindID. Two tiles with the same color ID will have the exact same texture pattern, adding a subliminal hint for matching.

    Matte Shine (LinearGradientPaint): A semi-transparent LinearGradient is layered on top to create a "glossy glint" without distorting the depth created by the radial gradient.

    Dynamic Click Ripple (Interaction focus):

    We override mousePressed to capture the click Point and reset the ripple state.

    The animation timer updates rippleRadius (making the circle expand) and rippleAlpha (making the ring fade out).

    The effect is drawn last, as a thick white ring that "explodes" outward from the point of impact.

    Clean Active/Hover Border: To fix the previous error, we now track isCurrentlyActive locally in the setActive override. When active, we draw a clean, bright white border.